win32u: SetFocus() into a hidden top-level of the thread that already owns the activation leaves the activation where it is (door 1 of 2 — see neutron-zzzzzzzzzzzzz-win32u-setparent-hidden-noactivate)

⚠️ **2026-09-17, later the same day: the "REFUTED" verdict below was WRONG and is retracted.** The
11.10-92 test shut THIS door and left a second one open: NtUserSetParent's reposition
(set_window_pos with SWP_NOSIZE and no SWP_NOACTIVATE) activates the same hidden host from Premiere's
main thread ~0.2 s later (premiere-20260916-185237.log, every cycle: `NtUserSetParent (0x30146 0x20144)`
→ `set_foreground_window hwnd 0x30146`), which is why "nothing changed". Both doors are shut by
`neutron-zzzzzzzzzzzzz-win32u-setparent-hidden-noactivate.mypatch`, which also flips this gate's
DEFAULT BACK TO 1 (the hunk lives in that patch; the diff below still shows the 0 it found). A/B for
the pair is in that patch's header. The block below is kept as history.

⛔ **THIS DOES NOT FIX THE CLICK-BACK FOCUS BUG. Refuted 2026-09-17, kept only as a recorded negative.**
Built as 11.10-92 and tested on `~/.premiere2025` with `NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1`: the guard fired correctly and
completely — 3 `neutron-setfocus-hidden … REFUSED` and **zero** `neutron-setfocus-fg`, i.e. the activation
steal it targets was PREVENTED OUTRIGHT — and the operator reported *"Nothing changed"*
(`premiere-20260917-143447.log`).

⭐ **That is a clean elimination and it is the value of this patch: the activation steal by the hidden CEP
host is NOT the mechanism of the click-back focus bug.** Do not re-open that line of attack. Prevent the
steal entirely and the bug is untouched.

**Therefore the gate defaults to 0.** It refuses `SetFocus` for hidden cross-thread windows — a real
behaviour change with no demonstrated benefit and a plausible way to break a CEP extension panel that
expects focus. Enable with `NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING=1` only to reproduce the negative.

⭐ **Observation from the same log, NOT a conclusion:** with the steal gone, every click-back still shows
TWO foreground transitions inside Premiere — focus arrives on the captioned popup that held it
(`kbdfg 0x20144 FOREGROUNDING`), and ~170 ms later the foreground is the main frame `0x10120`. Our own
`neutron-kbdfg` patch in winewayland `keyboard_handle_enter` performs the first. That double transition is
the next lead; it has not been tested.

--- original rationale below, still accurate as description, wrong as diagnosis ---


Premiere Pro: click away to another app, click back, and about 0.65 s later the app loses focus
and regains it. Operator: "this is a legitimate focus, unfocus, refocus process when clicking
back on the app." Not intermittent; every click-back cycle. Tracks CEP extension hosts:
~/Neutron-Prefixes/Adobe2026 runs 0 CEPHtmlEngine and never shows it, ~/.premiere2025 runs 10-34
and shows it every cycle. Ranked #6 in RELEASE-CANDIDATE.md.

MEASURED (premiere-20260917-134054.log, NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 WINEDEBUG=+timestamp, 11.10-91):

    1126.016  neutron-wlkillfg 0x50288 DEACTIVATING            <- click away (frame 0x50288)
    1127.718  neutron-kbdfg    0x50288 FOREGROUNDING           <- click back
    1127.741  neutron-role 0x2001b6 PLUGPLUG_UI_NATIVE_WINDOW  style 04c80000 (hidden, owner=frame)
    1127.745  neutron-role 0x60148  4b111f37-... style 86000000 VIS=0x0 owner=(nil) role=NONE
    1128.378  neutron-setfocus-fg hwnd=0x1002bc top=0x60148 (thread 1a2c, not ours)
              -> moved the foreground with the activation, was active 0x50288
    1128.644  dcomp REGISTER chain: 0x1002BC[Chrome_WidgetWin_0,pid1792] <- 0x1A0284[CefBrowserWindow]
              <- 0x60148[GUID host, pid Premiere] <- 0x2001B6[PLUGPLUG_UI_NATIVE_WINDOW] <- desktop

Every cycle: PlugPlug creates a 0x0, WS_POPUP, never-shown host window on Premiere's main thread,
a CEPHtmlEngine process parents a visible Chrome_WidgetWin_0 into it and runs Chromium's stock
activate sequence -- SetForegroundWindow(child), refused because WS_CHILD, then SetFocus(child).
NtUserSetFocus walks the child up to the hidden host and activates it, and the window the user is
looking at is deactivated. 11.10-90 without the foreground pairing (premiere-20260916-185237.log,
+win): "0a10 set_active_window hwnd 0x30146, previous 0x10156, mouse 0, focus 0". Same steal, same
source, also while extension panels load at startup (134054: 1115.590, 1120.111, 1120.284).

Windows comparison (HANDOFF-61, Win11 26200, read-only instruments): Windows performs the SAME
activation. Its control program (rc-scripts/ncact_child.py, T6) had a second process parent a
WS_CHILD|WS_VISIBLE into a hidden 0x86000000 popup and run exactly that sequence:

    [child proc] SetForegroundWindow(child) -> 0 err=0
    [child proc] SetFocus(child) -> prev=1838536
    STATE T6   fg=B active=B focus=B | ACTIVECAPTION: A=0 B=1

and real Premiere on Windows moved fg/active to the GUID host on 13 of 13 click-backs, for
240-320 ms. So the steal is not the divergence. What the user sees on Windows is nothing, for one
reason: Premiere's frame WndProc (in dvaworkspace.dll), on WM_NCACTIVATE,
forces wParam=TRUE whenever the window taking the activation is on its own thread,
so the frame's WS_ACTIVECAPTION bit stayed 1 through all 13 steals and a 60 Hz screen sampler saw
zero inactive frames. Wine answers every primitive that handler uses identically and the frame's
caption stays active under Wine too: 185237 paints "nc_paint 0x10120 4" through every steal and
"nc_paint 0x10120 0" only on the genuine click-away.

What the shield does NOT cover is every window the application does not subclass. Wine's comdlg32
#32770 file dialog is the measured case: with the Open dialog up, each steal paints
"nc_paint 0x10272 0" and the dialog's caption goes inactive and back (premiere-20260915-134050.log,
three cycles, 4 -> 0 -> 4). Every flicker-confirmed capture without a foreground move
(20260916-161220 on 11.10-90, 20260916-200445 with NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_FOREGROUND=0) ran its cycles
with a #32770 dialog (0x20156 / 0x30142, style 96cc20c4) as the foreground window. Whatever else
in the application reacts to WM_ACTIVATE(WA_INACTIVE) / WM_KILLFOCUS / the foreground leaving the
window it is showing is unshielded in the same way.

Fix: the application declares, in that shield, that activation moving to a hidden sibling on its
own thread is not a user-visible activation change. Apply the same rule one level down, at the
only entry point the hosts use. In NtUserSetFocus, when the resolved top-level is not already
active, the caller is not the thread that owns it, it has no WS_VISIBLE, and the activation
already belongs to its thread, leave the activation where it is and fail the call (return 0, as
Windows does when the focus cannot be set). Nothing can be typed into a window nobody can see, so
the refused focus has no observable owner.

What the guard excludes, and why the other apps cannot regress:
  - a thread activating its own hidden window before showing it (same thread) -- upstream path;
  - a hidden host that has been docked into a visible panel: it becomes WS_CHILD of the frame and
    the WS_CHILD walk then resolves to the frame, already active (134054 1121.273:
    "hwnd=0x3019c top=0x50288") -- never reaches this branch, so visible CEP panels (Captioneer,
    Photoshop/AE/Illustrator/Animate extension panels) keep their focus handling;
  - an activation coming from a thread whose windows do not hold the activation (another
    application's window, a dialog on a worker thread) -- upstream path;
  - active == 0 (nothing active, e.g. after our own click-away deactivation) -- upstream path.
  Photoshop, After Effects, Illustrator, Animate, Lightroom Classic and Media Encoder reach this
  branch only through the same CEP/CEF host pattern, where the refused target is by construction a
  window with no WS_VISIBLE.

This is a Neutron rule, not a restored Win32 semantic: Windows really does move the activation to
the hidden host and lets Premiere's own handler hide the consequence. The rule reproduces Windows'
visible outcome for every window the application does not shield. An earlier draft of the same
idea (11.10-91 development) was dropped unbuilt and untested when HANDOFF-61 showed Windows
activates the host; this one is written against that measurement, not in ignorance of it.

Kill switch NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a rebuild;
NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints one neutron-setfocus-hidden line per refusal, at the outcome. With the
gate on, the neutron-setfocus-fg line of the 11.10-91 patch no longer fires for these hosts (the
branch is never reached); with it off, it fires as before. A/B on ONE prefix (~/.premiere2025):
same gesture, gate on vs NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING=0, compare the operator's per-cycle
report against the neutron-setfocus-hidden / neutron-setfocus-fg lines.

--- a/dlls/win32u/input.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/input.c
@@ -2262,6 +2262,74 @@
     return TRUE;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: SetFocus() from another thread into a HIDDEN top-level of the thread that already
+ * owns the activation.
+ *
+ * Premiere's CEP extension hosts (PlugPlug) create a 0x0, WS_POPUP, never-shown host window on
+ * the application's main thread and hand it to a CEPHtmlEngine (CEF) process, which parents a
+ * visible Chrome_WidgetWin_0 into it and runs Chromium's stock activate sequence:
+ * SetForegroundWindow(child) -- refused, WS_CHILD -- then SetFocus(child). The WS_CHILD walk
+ * above resolves hwndTop to that hidden host, and activating it takes the activation away from
+ * the window the user is looking at: the main frame, or whatever dialog is up. Premiere does this
+ * on every click-back into the app, ~0.65 s after the click (premiere-20260917-134054.log), once
+ * per extension host spawned, and again while extension panels load.
+ *
+ * Windows performs the same activation (HANDOFF-61 T6, Win11 26200: the second process's SetFocus
+ * leaves fg == active == focus == the hidden popup for 240-320 ms). What the user sees on Windows
+ * is nothing, for one reason only: Premiere's frame WndProc (in dvaworkspace.dll), on
+ * WM_NCACTIVATE, forces wParam=TRUE whenever the window
+ * taking the activation is one of its own thread's windows, so its caption never paints inactive.
+ * Wine answers every primitive in that handler the same way and the frame's caption stays active
+ * under Wine too (premiere-20260916-185237.log: nc_paint <frame> 4 through every steal). What is
+ * NOT shielded on either platform is every window the application does not subclass -- Wine's
+ * comdlg32 #32770 file dialog above all (nc_paint 0x10272 0 inside each steal,
+ * premiere-20260915-134050.log) -- and whatever else in the application reacts to WM_ACTIVATE /
+ * WM_KILLFOCUS / the foreground moving away from the window it is showing.
+ *
+ * The application itself declares, in that shield, that activation moving to a hidden sibling on
+ * its own thread is not a user-visible activation change. This applies the same rule one level
+ * down, at the only entry point the hosts use: when the caller is not the thread that owns the
+ * target, the target has no WS_VISIBLE, and the activation already belongs to the target's
+ * thread, the activation is left where it is and SetFocus fails (returns 0, as it does on Windows
+ * when the focus cannot be moved). Nothing can be typed into a window nobody can see, so the
+ * refused focus has no observable owner. A hidden host that is later docked into a panel becomes
+ * WS_CHILD of the frame (the walk then resolves to the frame, already active -- 134054 line
+ * 1121.273 'top=0x50288'), so visible extension panels never reach this branch; nor does a thread
+ * activating its own hidden window before showing it (same thread), nor an activation coming from
+ * another application's thread (activation not already on the target's thread).
+ *
+ * This is a Neutron rule, not a restored Win32 semantic: Windows really does move the activation
+ * to the hidden host and lets Premiere's own handler hide the consequence. The rule reproduces
+ * Windows' visible outcome for every window the application does not shield.
+ *
+ * Kill switch NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a
+ * rebuild; NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints one neutron-setfocus-hidden line per refusal, at the outcome. */
+static BOOL neutron_setfocus_hidden_sibling_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+
+    if (on < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING" );
+        on = e ? (e[0] != '0') : 0;   /* NEUTRON: OFF by default -- refuted 2026-09-17, see header */
+        ERR( "neutron-gateresolved: NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_HIDDEN_SIBLING = %d (%s)\n", on, e ? "environment" : "default" );
+    }
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_setfocus_refuse_hidden_sibling( HWND top, HWND active )
+{
+    DWORD top_thread;
+
+    if (!top || !active) return FALSE;              /* nothing is active: upstream path */
+    if (!neutron_setfocus_hidden_sibling_on()) return FALSE;
+    if (get_window_long( top, GWL_STYLE ) & WS_VISIBLE) return FALSE;   /* a window the user can see */
+    top_thread = get_window_thread( top, NULL );
+    if (top_thread == GetCurrentThreadId()) return FALSE;   /* a thread activating its own window */
+    if (top_thread != get_window_thread( active, NULL )) return FALSE;  /* activation is elsewhere */
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 HWND WINAPI NtUserSetFocus( HWND hwnd )
 {
     HWND hwndTop = hwnd, active;
@@ -2302,6 +2370,18 @@
         if (!(active = get_active_window()) && !set_foreground_window( hwndTop, FALSE, FALSE )) return 0;
         if (hwndTop != active)
         {
+            if (neutron_setfocus_refuse_hidden_sibling( hwndTop, active ))
+            {
+                /* the activation stays on the window the user is looking at; the focus cannot
+                 * move outside the active window, so the call fails as it would on Windows when
+                 * the focus cannot be set */
+                if (neutron_wl_diag())
+                    ERR( "neutron-setfocus-hidden: hwnd=%p top=%p is hidden (style %08x) on thread %04x, which owns the active window %p -> activation left in place, SetFocus REFUSED (caller thread %04x)\n",
+                         hwnd, hwndTop, (unsigned int)get_window_long( hwndTop, GWL_STYLE ),
+                         (unsigned int)get_window_thread( hwndTop, NULL ), active,
+                         (unsigned int)GetCurrentThreadId() );
+                return 0;
+            }
             if (neutron_setfocus_moves_foreground( hwndTop, active ))
             {
                 /* set_foreground_window() activates hwndTop itself, keeping the pair in step */
